Actually, let me reconsider the whole approach. Maybe I should use a different trigger. Let me re-read the vulnerability description.

The vulnerability is a heap-buffer-overflow (WRITE 8) in `opj_j2k_read_tile_header`. The write is to `tp_index[l_current_tile_part]`. The fix guards with `tp_index && l_current_tile_part < nb_tps`.

So the overflow happens when `l_current_tile_part >= nb_tps` (or `tp_index` is NULL).

Let me think about when `tp_index` could be NULL. If `opj_j2k_build_tp_index_from_tlm` fails, `m_tlm.m_is_invalid` is set to true. Then in the SOT handler, `tp_index` is allocated. So `tp_index` is not NULL.

But what if `opj_j2k_build_tp_index_from_tlm` succeeds but doesn't allocate `tp_index` for a particular tile? Let me check.

In `opj_j2k_build_tp_index_from_tlm`:
```c
for (i = 0; i < l_tlm->m_entries_count; ++i) {
    OPJ_UINT32 l_tile_index_no = l_tlm->m_tile_part_infos[i].m_tile_index;
    ...
    ++p_j2k->cstr_index->tile_index[l_tile_index_no].current_nb_tps;
}
```
This counts the TLM entries per tile. Then:
```c
for (i = 0; i < p_j2k->cstr_index->nb_of_tiles; ++i) {
    if (p_j2k->cstr_index->tile_index[i].current_nb_tps == 0) {
        ... goto error;
    }
}
```
This requires all tiles to have at least 1 TLM entry. If any tile has 0 entries, the build fails.

Then:
```c
for (i = 0; i < l_tlm->m_entries_count; ++i) {
    OPJ_UINT32 l_tile_index_no = l_tlm->m_tile_part_infos[i].m_tile_index;
    opj_tile_index_t* l_tile_index = &(p_j2k->cstr_index->tile_index[l_tile_index_no]);
    if (!l_tile_index->tp_index) {
        l_tile_index->tp_index = (opj_tp_index_t *) opj_calloc(l_tile_index->current_nb_tps, sizeof(opj_tp_index_t));
        ...
    }
    ...
    l_tile_index->tp_index[l_tile_index->nb_tps].start_pos = l_cur_offset;
    ...
    ++l_tile_index->nb_tps;
    l_cur_offset += l_tlm->m_tile_part_infos[i].m_length;
}
```

So `tp_index` is allocated for each tile that has TLM entries. Since all tiles have at least 1 entry, `tp_index` is allocated for all tiles.

OK so `tp_index` is never NULL when TLM is valid. The overflow happens when `l_current_tile_part >= nb_tps`.

